        excerpt: "Behind every saying of the Prophet lies one of history's most rigorous systems of verification and scholarly care.",
        content: `
            <h2>The Second Source of Islam</h2>
            <p>The Quran is the first source of Islamic guidance, and the <strong>Sunnah</strong>, the recorded sayings, actions, and approvals of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), is the second. A hadith is a report of something the Prophet said, did, or silently approved. Together, Quran and Sunnah explain, detail, and bring to life the message of Islam.</p>

            <h2>From the Prophet's Tongue to the Scholars' Books</h2>
            <p>In the earliest years, the companions memorised the Prophet's words with the same devotion they gave to the Quran. They taught these narrations to the next generation, the tabi'in, who in turn passed them on. In this way, every hadith travelled through a visible chain of narrators before it ever reached a written collection, and that chain could be examined, tested, and judged.</p>

            <div class="quote-box">
                <p class="mb-0 fw-semibold">"Whoever tells a lie against me deliberately, then let him take his seat in the Fire." — Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim</p>
            </div>

            <h2>The Chain of Narration (Isnad)</h2>
            <p>The science of hadith revolves around two elements: the chain of narrators (isnad) and the text (matn). Scholars scrutinised every link in the chain. Was this narrator known to have met that one? Was the narrator trustworthy, precise, and free from weak memory? Could any link be missing, forged, or confused? Any doubt in the chain was enough to reduce the hadith's rank.</p>

            <h2>Classifying the Narrations</h2>
            <p>Based on this painstaking examination, scholars classified hadith as authentic (sahih), good (hasan), or weak (da'if), with further categories for reports that were rejected outright. This vocabulary of authenticity protected the religion from the interpolation of fabrications and gave later generations the tools to distinguish the Prophet's genuine words from later additions.</p>

            <h2>The Great Compilations</h2>
            <p>The most celebrated collections are those of Imam Bukhari and Imam Muslim, whose two books are regarded as the most authentic after the Quran. Imam Bukhari is said to have sifted through six hundred thousand narrations before accepting just over seven thousand. The precision, patience, and piety of these scholars is itself one of the great achievements of Islamic civilisation.</p>
            <p>The science of hadith is a standing proof of Islam's care for authenticity. While other traditions accumulated their scriptures over centuries with unknown authors, every line of the Prophet's teaching was examined, weighed, and verified through chains of known and trusted people. That diligence is itself a miracle of preservation.</p>
        `
