        content: `
            <h2>Leadership After the Prophet</h2>
            <p>When the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) passed away in 632 CE, the young Muslim community had to choose its leader. The companions agreed to follow the principle of consultation (shura), and Abu Bakr was chosen as the first caliph, meaning successor. The age of the four rightly guided caliphs, which lasted about thirty years, is regarded as the golden standard of Islamic leadership.</p>

            <div class="quote-box">
                <p class="mb-0 fw-semibold">"I advise you to fear Allah, to listen and obey, even if an Abyssinian slave is placed in authority over you. Whoever among you lives after me will see much disagreement, so adhere to my Sunnah and the way of the rightly guided caliphs after me; bite onto it with your molar teeth." — Sunan Abi Dawud and Sunan at-Tirmidhi</p>
            </div>

            <h2>Abu Bakr as-Siddiq</h2>
            <p>Abu Bakr, the closest friend of the Prophet and the first adult male to accept Islam, was known as as-Siddiq, the one who confirms the truth. During his brief two-year rule, he suppressed the apostasy movements that followed the Prophet's death, dispatched armies to spread the message, and ordered the first compilation of the Quran. He is remembered above all for his firmness and his faith.</p>

            <h2>Umar ibn al-Khattab</h2>
            <p>Umar, previously one of Islam's fiercest opponents, became one of its greatest statesmen. Under his ten-year rule the Islamic state expanded across Syria, Egypt, and Persia, and he established a system of governance, a treasury, courts, and an annual calendar. He walked the streets at night checking on his people, and history records no ruler more attentive to the welfare of the governed.</p>

            <h2>Uthman ibn Affan</h2>
            <p>Uthman, the third caliph, was known for his gentleness and generosity, having spent his wealth freely in the cause of Islam. His greatest legacy was the standardisation of the Quranic text and its distribution to the far corners of the empire, which united the ummah upon a single reading and preserved the scripture for all generations to come.</p>

            <h2>Ali ibn Abi Talib</h2>
            <p>Ali, the cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet, was raised in the Prophet's house and was renowned for his knowledge, eloquence, and courage. His caliphate was troubled by civil strife, yet his letters on governance and his emphasis on justice remain masterpieces of leadership. His period ended the age of the rightly guided caliphs and closed a unique chapter in Islamic history.</p>

            <h2>A Living Example</h2>
            <p>The four caliphs differed in temperament but shared one quality: they ruled for the sake of Allah and counted themselves servants of the people. They slept on simple mats, wore mended clothes, and answered for every coin of public money. Their lives remain a standing proof that leadership and humility can, and should, be one and the same.</p>
        `
