        content: `
            <h2>The Family as the Cornerstone of Society</h2>
            <p>Islam regards the family as the basic unit of society and the first school of faith and character. A healthy community can only be built upon healthy homes, and the Quran and Sunnah give detailed guidance for every relationship within the family: the bond of marriage, the rights of children, the honour due to parents, and the ties of kinship.</p>

            <div class="quote-box">
                <p class="mb-0 fw-semibold">"And of His signs is that He created for you from yourselves mates that you may find tranquillity in them; and He placed between you affection and mercy. Indeed in that are signs for a people who give thought." — Surah Ar-Rum (30:21)</p>
            </div>

            <h2>The Rights of the Spouse</h2>
            <p>Marriage in Islam is a solemn covenant, and both spouses have rights and responsibilities before Allah. The husband is responsible for providing, and the wife is entrusted with the home; but the Quran commands kindness in their dealings and the Prophet said the best of men are those who treat their wives best. The believers "are garments for one another," each covering and protecting the other.</p>

            <h2>The Rights of Children</h2>
            <p>Children enter this world with rights: the right to a good name, to education, to love and mercy, and to justice between siblings. The Prophet said every child is born upon the natural disposition (fitrah), and it is the parents who shape the child's path. He also warned parents not to make preference between children, for justice in the home is a trust.</p>

            <h2>The Rights of Parents</h2>
            <p>The Quran places kindness to parents immediately after the worship of Allah: "And your Lord has decreed that you not worship except Him, and to parents, good treatment" (Surah Al-Isra 17:23). Children must honour their parents in their lifetime, care for them in old age, and pray for them after death. Paradise, the Prophet said, lies beneath the feet of mothers.</p>

            <h2>Bonds Beyond the Home</h2>
            <p>Islam extends the duty of care to the wider family: maintaining ties of kinship, visiting the sick, supporting relatives in need, and keeping even the far cousins within the circle of mercy. The Prophet said the one who severs family ties will not enter paradise, while the one who maintains them finds his provision expanded and his life extended.</p>

            <h2>A Source of Peace</h2>
            <p>When every member of the family understands their role, the home becomes a sanctuary of tranquillity, which is exactly the description Allah gives of marriage. The family is where children first taste mercy, where the weak are protected, and where the elderly are honoured. It is the training ground of the society, and no reform of society can succeed that does not begin in the home.</p>
        `
